This is a known breaking change. Android deprecated `AdjustResize` (API 30+), and .NET MAUI 10 no longer shims it. The modern fix uses **Android's WindowInsets API** to react to the keyboard (IME) and adjust padding.

### Quick fix — two steps:

**1. `Platforms/Android/MainActivity.cs`** — opt into edge-to-edge and listen for keyboard insets:

```csharp
using Android.Views;
using AndroidX.Core.View;
using Microsoft.Maui;

[Activity(/* existing attributes */,
    WindowSoftInputMode = SoftInput.AdjustNothing)]
public class MainActivity : MauiAppCompatActivity
{
    protected override void OnCreate(Bundle? savedInstanceState)
    {
        base.OnCreate(savedInstanceState);

        // Enable edge-to-edge so insets are reported
        WindowCompat.SetDecorFitsSystemWindows(Window!, false);

        var rootView = Window!.DecorView.FindViewById(Android.Resource.Id.Content)!;
        ViewCompat.SetOnApplyWindowInsetsListener(rootView, new KeyboardInsetsListener());
    }

    class KeyboardInsetsListener : Java.Lang.Object, IOnApplyWindowInsetsListener
    {
        public WindowInsetsCompat OnApplyWindowInsets(View v, WindowInsetsCompat insets)
        {
            var imeInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.Ime());
            var navInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.NavigationBars());

            // Use whichever is taller — keyboard or nav bar
            int bottom = Math.Max(imeInsets.Bottom, navInsets.Bottom);
            v.SetPadding(0, 0, 0, bottom);

            return insets;
        }
    }
}
```

**2. XAML layout** — use a `Grid` with your input pinned to the bottom:

```xml
<Grid RowDefinitions="*, Auto">
    <CollectionView Grid.Row="0" ItemsSource="{Binding Messages}" ... />

    <HorizontalStackLayout Grid.Row="1" Padding="8">
        <Entry Placeholder="Type a message..." HorizontalOptions="FillAndExpand" />
        <Button Text="Send" />
    </HorizontalStackLayout>
</Grid>
```

### Why this works

| Old approach | New approach |
|---|---|
| `AdjustResize` told the OS to shrink the window | `WindowInsets` tells **your app** how much space the keyboard occupies |
| OS-driven, deprecated API 30+ | App-driven, forward-compatible |

### iOS

On iOS this isn't needed — MAUI still handles keyboard avoidance automatically. If you do see issues, add `KeyboardAutoManagerScroll.Disconnect()` in `AppDelegate` and manage scrolling yourself.
